employeeMapper.updateEntity(request, employee);

Much cleaner.

Option 4: Jakarta Bean Validation + Custom Constraint (Best for large projects)

This is what many enterprise applications use.

You create custom annotations like:

@ValidEmailIfPresent
private String email;

or

@NotBlankIfPresent
private String employeeName;

Then Spring validates automatically.

We'll cover this once you're comfortable with the basics.

What would I do in a real company?

If there are 20 fields:

15 are simple validations (blank, length, email)
5 are business validations (duplicate email, joining date, etc.)

I'd split them.

Bean Validation handles the simple rules.

Service handles business rules.

For our project

Since you're learning, I don't want to introduce too much magic too early.

We'll first build it manually so you understand why validation happens.

Then I'll show you how Spring can remove 80% of the boilerplate.

By the end, you'll appreciate the framework instead of just memorizing annotations.

Here's a question for you

Suppose the PATCH request is:

{
    "email": "abc@gmail.com"
}

Should Spring validate employeeName?

Answer: No, because the client isn't trying to update it.

This is exactly why PATCH validation is different from POST validation, and why we don't simply copy the create DTO annotations onto the update DTO. Understanding that distinction is more important than memorizing any specific validation technique.

